## Method
All steps run in the `gars-bio` conda environment (python; pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2,
pydeseq2 0.5.4, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1 — all pinned in
`_references/gars-bio.lock.txt`). One script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, submitted as one batch
job. Inputs are read only; nothing is written outside this analysis directory.

1. **Resolve inputs** at run time with
   `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`;
   the script reads the paths it returns, not the paths in the table above.
2. **Load counts.** Read the `counts_gene` TSV; index by `gene_id`, drop the `gene_name`
   column. Sample columns are A1, A2, A3, B1,
   B2, B3.
3. **Load design.** Read the `design` CSV; index by `sample_id`. Check that the set of sample
   columns in the counts equals the set of `sample_id`s in the design and abort with an error
   if not. Reorder count columns to the design's row order.
4. **Prepare counts.** The salmon length-scaled counts are non-integer; round to the nearest
   integer (the standard treatment of this nf-core file for DESeq2-style models). Drop genes
   whose count is zero in all six samples.
5. **Variance-stabilising transform.** Build a `pydeseq2.dds.DeseqDataSet` from the
   samples x genes integer matrix with the design's `condition` column as metadata and design
   `~condition`, then call `dds.vst(use_design=False)` — a blind VST, so the condition labels do
   not shape the transform that is supposed to show whether the conditions separate. Size
   factors use pydeseq2's default median-of-ratios.
6. **Feature selection.** Rank genes by variance of their VST values across samples and keep
   the top 500 (DESeq2's `plotPCA` default). This project's matrix has fewer than 500 genes, so
   in practice every retained gene is used; the number actually used is written to the
   summary table.
7. **PCA.** `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` on the samples x selected-genes VST matrix, centred
   (sklearn's default), not scaled; `n_components = min(n_samples, n_genes)` (= 6 here).
   Record each sample's score on every component and each component's explained variance
   ratio.
8. **Plot.** matplotlib scatter of PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample, coloured by `condition`
   (two colours, with a legend), each point labelled with its `sample_id`; axis labels give the
   percentage of variance explained (e.g. `PC1 (xx.x%)`). Saved as PNG at 300 dpi and as PDF.

